  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="law">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a gas safety certificate in St Helens?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If you rent out a home with gas, yes. Every gas appliance you supply must be checked every 12 months by a Gas Safe registered engineer.</p>
      <p>The rules come from the <a href="https://www.hse.gov.uk/gas/landlords/index.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gas Safety (Installation and Use) Regulations 1998</a>.</p>
      <h3>What landlords must do</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Get a new gas safety check every 12 months.</li>
        <li>Give your tenants a copy within 28 days of the check.</li>
        <li>Give new tenants a copy before they move in.</li>
        <li>Keep each record for at least 2 years.</li>
        <li>Fit a carbon monoxide alarm in any room with a gas boiler or other fixed fuel-burning appliance.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Renting out for the first time?</h3>
      <p>Buy-to-let is growing in St Helens, mainly in WA10 and WA12. Many first-time landlords are letting a home they once lived in, or one they inherited. The certificate must be done before your first tenant moves in. It must cover every gas appliance you leave in the house.</p>
      <div class="brx-note"><p><strong>Changing tenants?</strong> If the old certificate will run out during the new tenancy, renew it first. Call us as soon as your tenant gives notice.</p></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="gas-fires">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Does the gas fire need to be on the certificate?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If you supply it, yes. We do not check gas fires, so tell us when you book. We can disconnect and cap it safely.</p>
      <p>The Victorian terraces in the town centre, Parr, Thatto Heath and Sutton were built with a fireplace in the front room. Many had coal fires swapped for gas fires years ago. Some still have a back boiler behind the fire. First-time landlords miss the gas fire more than anything else.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Do not need the fire?</h3>
          <p>We disconnect and cap it safely. It then no longer needs to be on the certificate.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Want to keep it?</h3>
          <p>It must be checked by an engineer registered for gas fires. We check the boiler, cooker and hob.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Still have a back boiler? We can take it out, cap the fire and fit a new combi. See <a href="/boiler-installation-st-helens">boiler installation in St Helens</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="checks">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What do you check?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We check every gas appliance you supply, the pipework and the air supply. The aim is to make sure nothing can leak gas or carbon monoxide.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>The gas supply</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>A tightness test to prove there are no leaks</li>
            <li>Gas pressure at the meter and each appliance</li>
            <li>The emergency control valve works</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Each appliance</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>It burns gas safely, with a flue gas test on the boiler</li>
            <li>The flue takes fumes outside</li>
            <li>There is enough fresh air</li>
            <li>Safety devices cut the gas off when they should</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p>Older WA9 and WA10 terraces often have blocked air vents. Older boilers with an open flue get a spillage test. This proves fumes go up the flue, not into the room. We also test the carbon monoxide alarm.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does it work?</h2>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Call or book online.</strong> Tell us the address and every gas appliance, including any gas fire. We give you a fixed price.</li>
        <li><strong>We arrange access.</strong> We agree a time with you, your tenant or your agent.</li>
        <li><strong>The check.</strong> A Gas Safe registered engineer checks everything. Most homes take under an hour.</li>
        <li><strong>Your certificate.</strong> We email the certificate the same day, ready to give to your tenant.</li>
        <li><strong>Next year.</strong> We remind you before it runs out, so you never miss a date.</li>
      </ol>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>Need a certificate in St Helens this week?</strong> Call and we will find the next free slot.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="fail">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>What happens if something fails?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We tell you straight away and explain what it means. Gas engineers use three labels, set by the gas industry.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Label</th><th>What it means</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Immediately dangerous</strong></td><td>It is a risk to life right now.</td><td>With your consent, we disconnect it. It must not be used until it is fixed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>At risk</strong></td><td>A fault could become dangerous.</td><td>With your consent, we turn it off. It should not be used until it is fixed.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Not to current standards</strong></td><td>It met older rules, but not today's.</td><td>It can stay in use. We note it on the certificate.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>If a repair is needed, we give you the price first. Nothing starts without your say-so. Once everything is safe, we issue the certificate.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="st-helens">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Where in St Helens do you cover?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">The whole borough and the edges of it. The price is the same in every postcode.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Town and central</h3>
          <p>Town centre and Eccleston WA10. Parr, Sutton, Thatto Heath and Clock Face WA9. The most Victorian terraces, gas fires and back boilers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Haydock and Rainford</h3>
          <p>Haydock and Rainford WA11, plus Billinge WN5. Mostly post-war homes, many with combi boilers.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Newton-le-Willows</h3>
          <p>Newton-le-Willows and Earlestown WA12, plus Golborne WA3. A growing buy-to-let area near the station.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <h3>Selling a St Helens home?</h3>
      <p>You do not need a certificate by law to sell a home you live in. But buyers often ask for one. A £100 check can answer their questions before they hold up the sale.</p>
      <div class="brx-note">
        <p><strong>Landlords also need an EICR.</strong> A gas safety certificate covers the gas. The electrics need an EICR at least every 5 years. Our partner firm <a href="https://www.idealelectricalsolutions.co.uk/eicr-st-helens">Ideal Electrical Solutions does landlord EICRs in St Helens</a> from £149 including VAT, NICEIC approved, with the certificate emailed within 24 hours.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
